Autism Spectrum Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, Stereotyped/repetitive behaviors, Insistence on sameness/routines

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-008
Reason for Visit: Acute severe behavioral meltdown with self-injurious behavior following an unexpected disruption to a rigid daily routine.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregivers
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute, uncontrollable behavioral crisis featuring self-injurious head-banging and extreme distress due to an environmental change. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to rule out occult physical illness driving the behavior, assess for closed head injury, and assist caregivers with immediate de-escalation before regular clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Caregiver reports: "The internet went down so he couldn't watch his specific show at 6 PM. He has been screaming and hitting his head against the wall for two hours."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known history of neurodevelopmental delays present in the early developmental period. Symptoms include persistent deficits in social-emotional reciprocity and deficits in nonverbal communicative behaviors used for social interaction. There are marked deficits in developing, maintaining, and understanding relationships. The patient exhibits stereotyped or repetitive motor movements (hand-flapping) and an extreme insistence on sameness and inflexible adherence to routines. There are highly restricted, fixated interests that are abnormal in intensity. The current crisis was triggered by an unexpected loss of internet access. This led to acute hyperreactivity to sensory input (crying at normal household sounds) and severe distress. The patient demonstrates a lack of spontaneous seeking to share enjoyment or interests with others, and there is clinically significant impairment in current functioning.
Functional Status: Highly dependent on caregivers for daily structure and emotional regulation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of physical injury to self; caregivers are physically exhausted and struggling to maintain safety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient is rocking forcefully in a corner, avoiding all eye contact, vocally distressed, hands covering ears.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam shows no focal deficits. Cranial examination reveals mild frontal erythema consistent with self-reported head-banging, but no lacerations, palpable hematomas, or signs of basilar skull fracture. Pupils are equal and reactive. Assessment confirms pronounced deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, active stereotyped/repetitive behaviors (rocking/flapping), and severe distress secondary to disrupted insistence on sameness/routines.
Environmental Observations: Router is visibly unplugged/broken. Room is dimly lit at caregiver's attempt to reduce sensory load.
Medication Reconciliation: On low-dose Risperidone for irritability; last dose given correctly this morning.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Autism Spectrum Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute sensory/routine crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to normal tympanic membranes on examination and absence of fever, confirming the distress is behavioral rather than infectious.

Traumatic Brain Injury - Ruled out/unlikely due to intact gross neurology, reactive pupils, and lack of altered consciousness beyond baseline neurodivergence.

Seizure Disorder (Post-ictal state) - Ruled out/unlikely due to continuous directed behavioral response to the environment without loss of bowel/bladder control or rhythmic tonic-clonic activity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a severe, prolonged behavioral meltdown involving self-injurious acts (head-banging) secondary to a break in a rigid routine. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the risk of traumatic brain injury. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out physical trauma from the self-harm, exclude acute occult infections (which frequently present as behavioral changes in ASD), and provide immediate clinical support to exhausted caregivers. Delaying the assessment posed an unacceptable risk of undiagnosed head injury or further dangerous escalation of self-injurious behavior.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Physical examination cleared major head trauma. Administered a single PRN dose of oral Lorazepam 0.5mg to facilitate acute de-escalation of the panic state.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a "First/Then" visual schedule. When routines fail, immediately present a card showing "First [Alternative Calming Activity], Then [Reward/Special Interest]". Reward successful transition with high-value positive reinforcement. This utilizes operant conditioning to build flexibility by making the new path predictably rewarding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to NDIS behavioral support specialist for updated management plan.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended obtaining a soft protective helmet for severe acute episodes.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days; advise early follow-up with regular pediatrician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient experiences a loss of consciousness, begins vomiting, exhibits unequal pupils, or if self-injury causes bleeding or cannot be physically stopped by caregivers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Assessment of head injury is clinical; occult intracranial pathology can occasionally present delayed symptoms.
Clinical Course: The acute distress will resolve as the central nervous system downregulates. Behavioral rigidity is a chronic feature of the diagnosis.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor closely for the next 24 hours. If the patient becomes unusually lethargic, difficult to wake, or starts vomiting, take them to the emergency department immediately for a head scan.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The distress is a genuine neurological overload response, not a tantrum. The small dose of medication will act as a circuit breaker for the nervous system.
Actionable Steps: Keep the room quiet and dimly lit. Do not force physical contact during the wind-down phase unless necessary for safety. Use the "First/Then" visual board tomorrow.

